#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322252 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7614bf0-1e7c-574b-b719-2688014f48d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322254} {:answer/id #uuid "bae8a6ed-0e5a-5cdf-b3ed-a8910f140309" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322255} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb2d506-793c-5a3d-b8ac-0efc2d553542" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322256} {:answer/id #uuid "905c6c4e-3fe6-5f97-a292-9c43daa5e840" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322257} {:answer/id #uuid "785a5215-204e-5370-b63f-8281b34f4e4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "905c6c4e-3fe6-5f97-a292-9c43daa5e840" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322257} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d4f01ec-b054-5eed-bf09-91ea80a8787c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "49e0b1b0-d9e2-5f20-a04d-af2c1aeb9c97" :question/math-academy-id "q-120268" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044176/assets/25717d521feeec25f7b4b722ca7b8a4ee9b5fdc8423bac06953e5f2adc8eef3d.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $t=\\lambda r+\\mu s$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044176/assets/d585c949d7de8cc3fa2d62bfb60990af12242c32ef3390e563f7e627844c5a88.png)\n\nIn $△ORT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OR=3|r|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OR}$ and $r$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OR}=3r.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $RT=2|s|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{RT}$ has the opposite direction to $s$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{RT}=-2s.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\overset{\\to }{OR}+\\overset{\\to }{RT} \\\\  & =3r-2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $t=\\lambda r+\\mu s=3r-2s.$ \n\nSince $s∦r,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =-2$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322259 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04f89674-d7af-5dc8-bfd9-2e087787aab9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\frac{2}{3}m+\\frac{1}{3}n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322261} {:answer/id #uuid "72a56613-fe00-52b3-b362-68b5b14d6ede" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\frac{1}{2}m+\\frac{1}{2}n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322262} {:answer/id #uuid "f98b29bb-af53-5033-85b6-2886693498ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\frac{5}{9}m+\\frac{4}{9}n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322263} {:answer/id #uuid "5adaac84-a815-509a-81a5-14473e6256df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\frac{1}{3}m+\\frac{2}{3}n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322264} {:answer/id #uuid "f1a1fce0-ac36-5b88-bd9c-087cb7e5f75e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\frac{4}{9}m+\\frac{5}{9}n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5adaac84-a815-509a-81a5-14473e6256df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\frac{1}{3}m+\\frac{2}{3}n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322264} :answer-field/id #uuid "c63444f6-473b-524b-9370-90dd340d3013" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b737947-533d-5a00-ac54-8d960f9a17b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-120303" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044176/assets/50b87e86bd2985e54ce0a8fe6b3e23986f7e1946a5284d0ae297d96b9cbc60f6.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=m,$ $\\overset{\\to }{ON}=n$ and $P$ divides $\\overset{―}{MN}$ in the ratio $MP:PN=2:1.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OP}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MP}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=m,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{MP}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OMN,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{MN} & =\\overset{\\to }{MO}+\\overset{\\to }{ON} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{ON} \\\\  & =-m+n \\\\  & =n-m.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $MP:PN=2:1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{MP}=\\frac{2}{3}\\overset{\\to }{MN}=\\frac{2}{3}(n-m).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OP} & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MP} \\\\  & =m+\\frac{2}{3}(n-m) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}m+\\frac{2}{3}n.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322272 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c328b4b7-da8e-5a4f-ae5f-7584bce68984" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c328b4b7-da8e-5a4f-ae5f-7584bce68984" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322274} :answer-field/id #uuid "04f09979-820c-588c-a537-148693d26410" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "65028b3c-06a7-5ff4-b830-82c5ce361a6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-367064" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044176/assets/80cedeceb8d377617af880cab1c383643e3cd79055eccce046b0418598897ac8.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=\\lambda a-2b,$ then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a-2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $-2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $-2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044176/assets/7e6923e8b8b774eeaa2f885ec6aa24bb03f07c547fa50e7145a4113a18e92e8a.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $-2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=2|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-2a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =-2b-2a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a-2b=-2a-2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2\n$$"}]}
